    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_54ee5e3edc8a1ac246ee5af613ba1db3df137bd956621ece90c6efec36fc9793",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The Kubert functional equation rigidifies K_s into even and odd directions, and the symmetric full Farey transform detects only the nonodd projection by mapping it to a scalar multiple of S_{s-1}. Polylogarithm, Hurwitz-zeta, Bernoulli, and Clausen formulas therefore give different coordinates for that same regular signal, while the singular log-sine and half-interval log-tangent criteria reach Chebyshev prime data through finite products, Mobius inversion, and, for tangent, a dyadic difference.",
